WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.853
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4431.

2
00:00:00.853 --> 00:00:01.705
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.705 --> 00:00:01.989
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alright.

4
00:00:01.989 --> 00:00:08.811
Today is March twenty-seventh, 2015, and I'm Chelsea Arseneault representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

5
00:00:08.811 --> 00:00:13.926
We're working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum, Bill Shearman, to document the history of Mossville [Louisiana].

6
00:00:13.926 --> 00:00:17.053
Today we're at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

7
00:00:17.053 --> 00:00:18.758
I'm here today with Coach Williams.

8
00:00:18.758 --> 00:00:21.316
Thank you so much for agreeing to be interviewed.

9
00:00:21.316 --> 00:00:23.021
It means a lot to us.

10
00:00:23.021 --> 00:00:25.295
This is going to be a short interview.

11
00:00:25.295 --> 00:00:27.000
About fifteen to twenty minutes.

12
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:27.538
<v WILLIAMS>Thank you.

13
00:00:27.538 --> 00:00:28.077
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes, sir.

14
00:00:28.077 --> 00:00:33.462
And we'll likely be in touch with you later to set up a longer interview if you're interested in continuing.

15
00:00:33.462 --> 00:00:34.000
<v WILLIAMS>Sure.

16
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:34.526
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

17
00:00:34.526 --> 00:00:40.316
Just for the record, can you please state your full name?

18
00:00:40.316 --> 00:00:41.895
<v WILLIAMS>LaSalle Clarence Williams.

19
00:00:41.895 --> 00:00:42.421
<v ARSENEAULT>LaSalle . . .

20
00:00:42.421 --> 00:00:44.000
<v WILLIAMS>Clarence Williams.

21
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.014
<v ARSENEAULT>And when and where were you born?

22
00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:51.454
<v WILLIAMS>I was born in Houston, Texas.

23
00:00:51.454 --> 00:00:53.174
June eighth, 1931.

24
00:00:53.174 --> 00:00:56.615
<v ARSENEAULT>And what are your parents’ names?

25
00:00:56.615 --> 00:00:59.482
<v WILLIAMS>Agnes Williams and Arthur Williams.

26
00:00:59.482 --> 00:01:00.055
<v ARSENEAULT>Agnes.

27
00:01:00.055 --> 00:01:04.642
What's your mother's maiden name, if you remember?

28
00:01:04.642 --> 00:01:08.656
<v WILLIAMS>No. Let's see . . . Yes, I might remember.

29
00:01:08.656 --> 00:01:19.550
I'm looking at her . . . we called her Teddy all the time . . . Nickname, but I can't recall her real name.

30
00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:22.417
Probably come to me later.

31
00:01:22.417 --> 00:01:30.445
But my biological parents, let me just throw this in, they gave me up.

32
00:01:30.445 --> 00:01:34.459
I was adopted into the Williams family.

33
00:01:34.459 --> 00:01:41.339
My biological mother . . . I never did know who my biological daddy was,

34
00:01:41.339 --> 00:01:47.073
but my . . . I always knew who my biological mother was.

35
00:01:47.073 --> 00:01:54.528
And so the Williams . . . my . . . The Williams family also raised my biological mother.

36
00:01:54.528 --> 00:02:03.702
Matter of fact and the whole family, and so I was adopted into the Williams family.

37
00:02:03.702 --> 00:02:11.156
My biological mother just passed not about eight years ago in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.

38
00:02:11.156 --> 00:02:16.890
They all moved from here and went to Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.

39
00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.757
They wanted me to go.

40
00:02:19.757 --> 00:02:25.491
I went up there and stayed several days, several . . . I

41
00:02:25.491 --> 00:02:34.092
<c.p>would go every summer, but since my ties was here with my . . . I call grandparents,

42
00:02:34.092 --> 00:02:43.839
but that was the ones that were adopting me and that’s where I been all my life.

43
00:02:43.839 --> 00:02:49.000
So that's how I got into the Williams.

44
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:53.150
<v ARSENEAULT>So what was your grandparents’ last names?

45
00:02:53.150 --> 00:03:00.264
<v WILLIAMS>My grandparent's last name was, I think it was [Hednots?] I believe.

46
00:03:00.264 --> 00:03:20.421
I've been trying to find out where they came . . . All these years never thought I was going to need this information but never know . . . I know one part of them came from Deridder [Louisiana].

47
00:03:20.421 --> 00:03:26.350
Now the other part, I don't know, somewhere down east.

48
00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:37.614
They had all these places in [Racatoe?] and all of them, didn’t know about all that area down there.

49
00:03:37.614 --> 00:03:44.729
But we never . . . They never really shared that with us that much.

50
00:03:44.729 --> 00:03:46.507
So, what's next?

51
00:03:46.507 --> 00:03:50.657
<v ARSENEAULT>What is your earliest memory of Mossville?

52
00:03:50.657 --> 00:04:00.143
<v WILLIAMS>My earliest memory Mossville, about going to school in Mossville, is where I attended elementary school.

53
00:04:00.143 --> 00:04:09.629
And I remember [clears throat] my daddy had a business, and it was a wood yard.

54
00:04:09.629 --> 00:04:12.000
<v ARSENEAULT>A wood yard?

55
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:12.480
<v WILLIAMS>Right.

56
00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.398
Selling wood like firewood.

57
00:04:14.398 --> 00:04:22.553
See back in those days, in this area we didn't have no fireplaces but everybody had woodstoves.

58
00:04:22.553 --> 00:04:25.431
Like just gas and all that

59
00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:29.268
<c.p>electric stove they didn't have, everything was woodstove.

60
00:04:29.268 --> 00:04:33.106
So wood was a valuable commodity back then.

61
00:04:33.106 --> 00:04:38.382
But he sold to mostly wealthy, white people who had fireplaces.

62
00:04:38.382 --> 00:04:42.220
And they had two wood yards in Mossville.

63
00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:46.057
Williams had one, my dad, and then Rigmaidens.

64
00:04:46.057 --> 00:04:47.976
That was two businesses.

65
00:04:47.976 --> 00:04:54.211
And my earliest memory is I learned how to use a crosscut saw.

66
00:04:54.211 --> 00:05:05.244
I cut so much wood with a crosscut saw, and broke so many bocks, and stacked wood, and I know all about that.

67
00:05:05.244 --> 00:05:09.561
Going to school, we had to walk to school.

68
00:05:09.561 --> 00:05:12.919
We didn't have no buses or nothing.

69
00:05:12.919 --> 00:05:15.797
School was located in this area.

70
00:05:15.797 --> 00:05:23.472
There's two . . . One building with two big rooms and they had a partition in the front.

71
00:05:23.472 --> 00:05:27.789
And that’s where I got my basic elementary education.

72
00:05:27.789 --> 00:05:34.504
I can remember the principal, the teachers, and that was here at that time.

73
00:05:34.504 --> 00:05:42.659
And in the afternoon when you got out of school, walk home, you had to do chores.

74
00:05:42.659 --> 00:05:51.293
And that was . . . Everybody had to do chores and they didn’t have no telephones or nothing like that,

75
00:05:51.293 --> 00:05:54.171
but people knew how to communicate.

76
00:05:54.171 --> 00:06:02.325
In other words, you did something somewhere, by time you got home your parents knew about it.

77
00:06:02.325 --> 00:06:10.000
So that was memories and not only that but chores that you had to do.

78
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.450
We . . . On weekends, we had recreation.

79
00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:15.307
We had our own little ball field.

80
00:06:15.307 --> 00:06:17.757
We didn't have no real ball.

81
00:06:17.757 --> 00:06:23.881
We make our balls out of rocks and tape, and rags, and sewed them up.

82
00:06:23.881 --> 00:06:32.046
And we had broom handles something like that as our bats and we had all kinds of fun with that.

83
00:06:32.046 --> 00:06:37.353
The other big game we had in Mossville for recreation was shooting marbles.

84
00:06:37.353 --> 00:06:41.436
See, and we played Indians and cowboys and all that.

85
00:06:41.436 --> 00:06:46.743
And interesting enough, you probably too young to know about the Tarzan movies.

86
00:06:46.743 --> 00:06:50.826
The Tarzan movies were real popular back in those days.

87
00:06:50.826 --> 00:06:51.234
Tarzan?

88
00:06:51.234 --> 00:06:56.541
And then these woods right there they had a lot of rattan vines.

89
00:06:56.541 --> 00:06:58.991
You don't know what that is.

90
00:06:58.991 --> 00:07:07.972
That's the things that what you call him would swing on in the jungle and we'd go play Tarzan in the woods.

91
00:07:07.972 --> 00:07:14.096
See with these . . . These are big old vines that grow high up and real strong.

92
00:07:14.096 --> 00:07:16.954
Swing all over the woods with them.

93
00:07:16.954 --> 00:07:20.628
So we had a lot of fun with that.

94
00:07:20.628 --> 00:07:23.078
Other things, crawfishing and all of

95
00:07:23.078 --> 00:07:23.486
<c.p>that.

96
00:07:23.486 --> 00:07:29.202
We . . . Then wasn't that, it was popular to us, but it wasn’t that valuable.

97
00:07:29.202 --> 00:07:32.468
We had a lot of fun in Mossville.

98
00:07:32.468 --> 00:07:39.000
But I . . . There's so many memories if you kind of kick something out to me . . .

99
00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:41.856
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did you live growing up?

100
00:07:41.856 --> 00:07:43.284
<v WILLIAMS>Where'd I what?

101
00:07:43.284 --> 00:07:46.140
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did you live growing up?

102
00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:47.092
<v WILLIAMS>Where did . . . ?

103
00:07:47.092 --> 00:07:49.472
<v ARSENEAULT>Can you describe the house?

104
00:07:49.472 --> 00:07:52.328
<v WILLIAMS>The house was . . . Had three bedrooms.

105
00:07:52.328 --> 00:07:56.136
My mom and dad lived in one room.

106
00:07:56.136 --> 00:08:03.276
Her mother lived with us in the middle room, and I had the back room.

107
00:08:03.276 --> 00:08:05.656
And the floors were wood.

108
00:08:05.656 --> 00:08:10.892
And the walls, we didn't have wallpaper like they have today.

109
00:08:10.892 --> 00:08:12.796
They took regular newspaper,

110
00:08:12.796 --> 00:08:25.648
and they would take this . . . I think it was starch and someone would mix it up and make a paste and paste your walls over with newspaper.

111
00:08:25.648 --> 00:08:28.504
We didn't have no air conditioning.

112
00:08:28.504 --> 00:08:32.312
You sleep at night with your windows open.

113
00:08:32.312 --> 00:08:35.644
We had screens naturally from the mosquitos.

114
00:08:35.644 --> 00:08:40.880
And our floors were just hardwood floors and naturally wood stoves.

115
00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:47.068
And my dad raised a lot of chickens, and ducks, and geese, guineas.

116
00:08:47.068 --> 00:08:55.636
Then plus not only did he have a wood yard, he had a big farm in the back.

117
00:08:55.636 --> 00:09:00.396
And he did all of this with two horses, a

118
00:09:00.396 --> 00:09:04.204
<c.p>[slide?], whatever them plows and all that, wheelbarrows.

119
00:09:04.204 --> 00:09:06.108
I remember those things.

120
00:09:06.108 --> 00:09:17.532
And he raised corn, beans, and everything just basically that he raised, that's what we ate and shared with other people in the community.

121
00:09:17.532 --> 00:09:18.008
Hogs.

122
00:09:18.008 --> 00:09:23.720
Well he did . . . He had everything and all that and we survived.

123
00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:25.624
Now my mama, Agnes,

124
00:09:25.624 --> 00:09:34.668
was the first woman that I know in Mossville that . . . she'd haul . . . You don't know nothing about sulfur mines,

125
00:09:34.668 --> 00:09:38.000
you may have heard about it.

126
00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:39.200
<v ARSENEAULT>What is it?

127
00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:40.000
<v WILLIAMS>Sulfur mines.

128
00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:42.400
That's where they would manufacture sulfur.

129
00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:46.000
That's how you, in Sulphur there was the mines.

130
00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:53.200
And we always . . . They always . . . I don't know how they did it but they always had a car,

131
00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:57.600
a Model T Ford or something; they always had a car.

132
00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:09.600
And she'd haul women from Mossville each day over there to work for the wealthy white folks in the restaurant business and that's what she did most of her life.

133
00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:14.000
And she always had an automobile, as a matter of fact.

134
00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:15.600
That's how I learned.

135
00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:21.600
I stole it one day and crossed the field and I learned how to drive.

136
00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:26.000
But she was quite an instrument in Mossville helping other people.

137
00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:30.000
But my dad was mostly around home, a hard worker.

138
00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:38.000
Now I understand somebody told me he used to gamble but I must have been too small to see that.

139
00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:45.200
When I grow up, when I got big enough, I never seen him do any of these things.

140
00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:48.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So your family had a garden?

141
00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:48.797
<v WILLIAMS>Oh, yeah.

142
00:10:48.797 --> 00:10:50.391
That was every year.

143
00:10:50.391 --> 00:10:53.579
Chickens . . . We didn't buy chickens we raised chickens.

144
00:10:53.579 --> 00:10:57.564
I done killed so many chicken by wringing their neck.

145
00:10:57.564 --> 00:10:58.361
And see

146
00:10:58.361 --> 00:11:03.143
<c.p>what they would do is . . . The chicken was all over the yard,

147
00:11:03.143 --> 00:11:08.722
but when they get ready to eat one they put them in the coop.

148
00:11:08.722 --> 00:11:11.113
I guess that's what they called.

149
00:11:11.113 --> 00:11:21.075
Clean them out for so many days and then our job was to go out there and kill them and wring the neck of them.

150
00:11:21.075 --> 00:11:23.865
They showed me how to pick them.

151
00:11:23.865 --> 00:11:26.256
The same thing with the ducks.

152
00:11:26.256 --> 00:11:29.842
Of course them guineas, I never could catch them.

153
00:11:29.842 --> 00:11:32.233
I don't remember dealing with guineas.

154
00:11:32.233 --> 00:11:39.008
But I tell you something else that always stick in my mind, I guess there's about nature.

155
00:11:39.008 --> 00:11:45.383
They had plum trees, fruit trees, you ain't never heard of nothing . . . You heard of muscadine?

156
00:11:45.383 --> 00:11:46.579
<v ARSENEAULT>Muscadine wine, yeah.

157
00:11:46.579 --> 00:11:52.955
<v WILLIAMS>Well we used to go in . . . these muscadines . . . Would go in the woods and collect them.

158
00:11:52.955 --> 00:11:59.331
Well we had fig trees, peach trees, and oh . . . I guess eight or nine plum trees.

159
00:11:59.331 --> 00:12:02.917
And they were I mean big old healthy plums.

160
00:12:02.917 --> 00:12:08.895
And all of a sudden this stuff just disappeared, and I think I know why.

161
00:12:08.895 --> 00:12:11.286
That's when industry started coming in.

162
00:12:11.286 --> 00:12:22.045
But just about everything that we . . . except for sugar and stuff like that you’d go to the store and get . . . and it might be interesting to know,

163
00:12:22.045 --> 00:12:25.632
you might want this in your . . . The big interview.

164
00:12:25.632 --> 00:12:28.421
They had one grocery store in Mossville.

165
00:12:28.421 --> 00:12:29.218
Only one.

166
00:12:29.218 --> 00:12:34.000
There was gas on that right there by the railroad track.

167
00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:35.455
<v ARSENEAULT>What was it called?

168
00:12:35.455 --> 00:12:36.545
<v WILLIAMS>Garrett's Grocery Store.

169
00:12:36.545 --> 00:12:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Garrett's Grocery Store.

170
00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:38.833
<v WILLIAMS>Ira Garrett.

171
00:12:38.833 --> 00:12:43.831
And you could go there and get sausage . . . Anything in the house.

172
00:12:43.831 --> 00:12:48.412
And what he used to do to get by on credit.

173
00:12:48.412 --> 00:12:54.243
You’d go there and they’d write it on the book and they pay it.

174
00:12:54.243 --> 00:13:00.490
Now, many times I had to go to the store with that bill to collect.

175
00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:05.071
And that was the only store that served the whole Mossville.

176
00:13:05.071 --> 00:13:10.902
And by that same area they had a post office one time in Mossville,

177
00:13:10.902 --> 00:13:17.149
across the street from it and right by the grocery store were the railroad tracks.

178
00:13:17.149 --> 00:13:19.231
They had a passenger train,

179
00:13:19.231 --> 00:13:27.144
and some people caught the train right there off of the . . . Get on the train and ride to Texas,

180
00:13:27.144 --> 00:13:29.227
wherever they had to go.

181
00:13:29.227 --> 00:13:30.059
Baton Rouge.

182
00:13:30.059 --> 00:13:34.224
So it's . . . Mossville have an interesting little history behind it.

183
00:13:34.224 --> 00:13:39.222
There were some other businesses, too, they have in Mossville besides that.

184
00:13:39.222 --> 00:13:42.554
They had a drugstore in Mossville one time.

185
00:13:42.554 --> 00:13:43.387
Purdy’s Drugstore.

186
00:13:43.387 --> 00:13:52.133
Now it was a sub . . . I’d say a sub-drugstore from the one she had in Lake Charles [Louisiana] on Mill Street.

187
00:13:52.133 --> 00:14:01.295
Then she opened one over here right down the street from where school is now, where Sasol [energy and chemical company] is.

188
00:14:01.295 --> 00:14:08.792
Now talk about clubs . . . I had a bunch of clubs like bars and all that over in Mossville.

189
00:14:08.792 --> 00:14:13.789
Had a hotel in Mossville one time right down Evergreen [Road], see.

190
00:14:13.789 --> 00:14:17.538
So I remember a lot of things about Mossville.

191
00:14:17.538 --> 00:14:20.037
You ever heard of Shoat’s Prairie?

192
00:14:20.037 --> 00:14:22.952
Well, somebody going to have it somewhere.

193
00:14:22.952 --> 00:14:28.366
It’s up in . . . He got his name from all of the wild hogs,

194
00:14:28.366 --> 00:14:33.364
and that’s right up there where Sasol is building some of their . . .

195
00:14:33.364 --> 00:14:34.613
<v ARSENEAULT>What is it?

196
00:14:34.613 --> 00:14:35.446
<v WILLIAMS>Shoat’s Prairie.

197
00:14:35.446 --> 00:14:38.778
<v ARSENEAULT>Is a shoat a kind of a pig?

198
00:14:38.778 --> 00:14:39.611
<v WILLIAMS>Wild hog.

199
00:14:39.611 --> 00:14:40.444
Wild pigs.

200
00:14:40.444 --> 00:14:43.359
And they'd run all over the place.

201
00:14:43.359 --> 00:14:46.275
People would kill them and eat them.

202
00:14:46.275 --> 00:14:49.190
Some people wouldn't because they was wild.

203
00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:55.437
A lot of people had a real regular number of hogs that they would raise.

204
00:14:55.437 --> 00:15:00.018
But these were hogs that they would run in the woods.

205
00:15:00.018 --> 00:15:03.767
And that’s where it got its name, Shoat’s Prairie.

206
00:15:03.767 --> 00:15:07.515
<v ARSENEAULT>Just for my own curiosity, were the hogs dangerous?

207
00:15:07.515 --> 00:15:09.181
They have those tusks?

208
00:15:09.181 --> 00:15:11.680
<v WILLIAMS>Well yeah, yeah they was dangerous.

209
00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:12.929
They'll cut you.

210
00:15:12.929 --> 00:15:15.844
Don't get too close to them, see.

211
00:15:15.844 --> 00:15:20.842
Most people were the ones that had guns but they'd trap them.

212
00:15:20.842 --> 00:15:23.757
See and then they would shoot them.

213
00:15:23.757 --> 00:15:29.172
But today I think that's what they do today, don't they trap them?

214
00:15:29.172 --> 00:15:35.419
They still got them in this area and they got them all around this area.

215
00:15:35.419 --> 00:15:40.000
In Carlyss [Louisiana], Moss Bluff [Louisiana], but they’re real dangerous.

216
00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:42.442
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did you go to church?

217
00:15:42.442 --> 00:15:45.292
<v WILLIAMS>I started church in the Methodist church.

218
00:15:45.292 --> 00:15:48.956
That's where I grew up, in the Methodist church.

219
00:15:48.956 --> 00:15:53.434
And it was located right at the corner of Prater Road.

220
00:15:53.434 --> 00:16:01.168
If you turn that corner, you see that open space there, well that's where I grew up in church.

221
00:16:01.168 --> 00:16:08.903
Now my parents . . . Well, my uncle and aunties all were Baptists and I was raised in the Methodist church.

222
00:16:08.903 --> 00:16:15.416
But I'm a Baptist today and somehow they recruited me from the Methodist to the Baptist.

223
00:16:15.416 --> 00:16:17.858
And I been there ever since.

224
00:16:17.858 --> 00:16:23.558
But this was something, and when I was growing up this was a must.

225
00:16:23.558 --> 00:16:24.779
Every Sunday you

226
00:16:24.779 --> 00:16:27.221
<c.p>going to wind up in church.

227
00:16:27.221 --> 00:16:29.664
No if and ands about it.

228
00:16:29.664 --> 00:16:37.398
You was going to go to church whether you learned anything or not you were going to be there,

229
00:16:37.398 --> 00:16:40.655
and the parents was going to bring you.

230
00:16:40.655 --> 00:16:42.690
But it's not like today.

231
00:16:42.690 --> 00:16:46.761
They go if they want, if they feel like it.

232
00:16:46.761 --> 00:16:53.274
I don't know how it is in your area, but I know that’s what I experienced.

233
00:16:53.274 --> 00:16:55.717
But then, that was a must.

234
00:16:55.717 --> 00:17:05.487
I don't care if you went out on Saturday night and stayed there until you was old enough, you going to go to church.

235
00:17:05.487 --> 00:17:09.558
You ain't going to be in no bed Sunday morning.

236
00:17:09.558 --> 00:17:16.478
My rule was, my daddy told me I had to be in the house at twelve o'clock.

237
00:17:16.478 --> 00:17:22.177
He said, “If you ain’t in this house . . .” That's when I was old enough.

238
00:17:22.177 --> 00:17:29.097
Said, “If you ain't in this house by twelve o'clock, boy, you ain't up to nothing good.”

239
00:17:29.097 --> 00:17:35.611
So, many times if I got caught out to twelve, I slip in the back room.

240
00:17:35.611 --> 00:17:42.938
I know how to get in the window and get in my bed without . . . They didn’t say nothing,

241
00:17:42.938 --> 00:17:46.195
so I bet they knew about it [laughs].

242
00:17:46.195 --> 00:17:48.230
But church was a must.

243
00:17:48.230 --> 00:17:52.301
I been a Baptist I guess over forty years now.

244
00:17:52.301 --> 00:17:59.221
So I left from . . . When I left the Methodist church I was superintendent of the Sunday school.

245
00:17:59.221 --> 00:18:10.212
So when they got me in the Baptist church they knew I had that background where I was superintendent of that for about fifteen years, I guess.

246
00:18:10.212 --> 00:18:21.204
I just decided that when I retired from education and I went and got this job at the sheriff's department . . . Well I didn’t have the time then.

247
00:18:21.204 --> 00:18:29.752
Of course I was working shift work and a lot of Sundays I wasn't at church because I had to work.

248
00:18:29.752 --> 00:18:36.265
So I gave up the Sunday school because of that, because I couldn't justify giving [?] so.

249
00:18:36.265 --> 00:18:44.000
Now I'm chairman of the deacon board and I been that for, I guess twenty years now, maybe.

250
00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:50.475
<v ARSENEAULT>So who were some of the leaders in the church when you were growing up?

251
00:18:50.475 --> 00:18:51.770
<v WILLIAMS>What is what?

252
00:18:51.770 --> 00:18:55.656
<v ARSENEAULT>Who were some of the leaders in the church?

253
00:18:55.656 --> 00:19:02.131
<v WILLIAMS>Well, most of the leaders then was the pastors and the deacons, and the deaconess.

254
00:19:02.131 --> 00:19:10.333
And of course the greatest ones that I know of in Mossville area was . . . Now these were different churches,

255
00:19:10.333 --> 00:19:12.923
you had only two main churches.

256
00:19:12.923 --> 00:19:17.672
One of them Sasol bought down here almost to the school.

257
00:19:17.672 --> 00:19:20.262
That was known as Sanctified Church.

258
00:19:20.262 --> 00:19:28.896
Then on the corner was a Methodist church, and then the church I'm at, Mount Zion, was a Baptist church.

259
00:19:28.896 --> 00:19:32.781
So that's the only three churches they had here.

260
00:19:32.781 --> 00:19:39.257
All these other churches came in through the years and that was the basic churches.

261
00:19:39.257 --> 00:19:46.596
Sanctified Church, the Methodist church, and the Baptist Church, but they all got along with each other.

262
00:19:46.596 --> 00:19:52.208
Somebody had an activity going they all went and helped the other church.

263
00:19:52.208 --> 00:19:55.230
It's not like today they’re all selfish.

264
00:19:55.230 --> 00:19:57.820
They’re all too busy about helping.

265
00:19:57.820 --> 00:19:59.546
But maybe something else.

266
00:19:59.546 --> 00:20:03.000
Ask me something else about the church.

267
00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:08.682
<v ARSENEAULT>Well, I was going to ask you where you went to school?

268
00:20:08.682 --> 00:20:09.155
<v WILLIAMS>School?

269
00:20:09.155 --> 00:20:12.943
Well I went to school here in Mossville.

270
00:20:12.943 --> 00:20:17.205
And you know that’s the time of the segregation.

271
00:20:17.205 --> 00:20:22.413
You don't know nothing about that; you probably read about it.

272
00:20:22.413 --> 00:20:24.307
But everything was segregated.

273
00:20:24.307 --> 00:20:35.198
Mossville went to the sixth grade and the idea was when you completed the sixth grade in Mossville, you done graduated from school.

274
00:20:35.198 --> 00:20:38.039
That's the furthest you could go.

275
00:20:38.039 --> 00:20:44.194
Then through the years, they went to one more grade, the seventh grade.

276
00:20:44.194 --> 00:20:53.191
So my parents, my biological parents, my parents was real anxious to see that I went to high school.

277
00:20:53.191 --> 00:20:56.505
<c.p>That I got a twelfth grade education.

278
00:20:56.505 --> 00:21:05.502
So when I completed my education here, the only high school they had in this area was Westlake High.

279
00:21:05.502 --> 00:21:07.869
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that for the white . . .?

280
00:21:07.869 --> 00:21:10.237
Was it a white school?

281
00:21:10.237 --> 00:21:11.184
<v WILLIAMS>White school.

282
00:21:11.184 --> 00:21:13.551
You know anything about Westlake?

283
00:21:13.551 --> 00:21:14.498
<v BILL SHEARMAN>Yes, sir.

284
00:21:14.498 --> 00:21:14.972
<v WILLIAMS>Alright.

285
00:21:14.972 --> 00:21:20.180
At that time Westlake High School was located where [?] eighty is.

286
00:21:20.180 --> 00:21:26.336
The main road from Mossville to Lake Charles passed right by that school.

287
00:21:26.336 --> 00:21:27.283
Lake Charles.

288
00:21:27.283 --> 00:21:30.597
Now that’s going over the old bridge.

289
00:21:30.597 --> 00:21:32.965
They had a drawbridge then.

290
00:21:32.965 --> 00:21:41.014
I had to pass by a white school to go to Lake Charles to get an education.

291
00:21:41.014 --> 00:21:47.643
So what happened, my biological mother loaded me up, and my brother and sister,

292
00:21:47.643 --> 00:21:55.693
and she drove us to Lake Charles which was known then as Second Ward on Mill Street.

293
00:21:55.693 --> 00:21:58.534
Now that was a city school.

294
00:21:58.534 --> 00:22:02.322
At that time you had two school districts.

295
00:22:02.322 --> 00:22:05.636
You had the parish and the city.

296
00:22:05.636 --> 00:22:11.792
Those people who paid tax in the city went to the city school.

297
00:22:11.792 --> 00:22:17.000
The ones outside couldn't go because they were not taxpayers.

298
00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:21.979
So therefore they tried to keep us out from going to school.

299
00:22:21.979 --> 00:22:29.862
So you had to find somebody in Lake Charles that you know as a relative and establish your address.

300
00:22:29.862 --> 00:22:38.989
Well, what they found out was we went on the Second Ward and we was going back and forth, nobody bothered us.

301
00:22:38.989 --> 00:22:47.702
But they had the black principal trying to catch us to turn us in but he never could really catch us.

302
00:22:47.702 --> 00:22:52.681
I don't think he tried that hard really, as I mature now.

303
00:22:52.681 --> 00:22:56.000
Because he could have easy caught us.

304
00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:59.494
<v ARSENEAULT>You don't think he wanted to catch y'all?

305
00:22:59.494 --> 00:23:01.677
<v WILLIAMS>I don't think so, really.

306
00:23:01.677 --> 00:23:09.975
But that was his assignment to make sure that nobody out in the parish would attend the city school.

307
00:23:09.975 --> 00:23:19.582
Well, what happened that same time, Second Ward, they built W. O. Boston, which was the high school right across the street.

308
00:23:19.582 --> 00:23:22.203
Well they had a football team.

309
00:23:22.203 --> 00:23:27.006
The other school didn't have nothing but basketball, football, and track.

310
00:23:27.006 --> 00:23:29.190
I went out for football.

311
00:23:29.190 --> 00:23:33.557
And what the coach did, he was from New Orleans,

312
00:23:33.557 --> 00:23:40.981
he lined everybody up and made us run two hundred yards to see who was the fastest.

313
00:23:40.981 --> 00:23:45.348
That's the way of picking out who his backs was.

314
00:23:45.348 --> 00:23:50.589
And unbelieving to me, I was thirty yards in front of everybody.

315
00:23:50.589 --> 00:23:54.956
So then they knew what kind of speed I had.

316
00:23:54.956 --> 00:24:05.000
I became a football player and once I became a football player, and I had the best track record in Lake Charles.

317
00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:16.658
There was the white coaches who were keeping up with me at Lake Charles High because we did all of our running on their track, see?

318
00:24:16.658 --> 00:24:23.833
So I know he was anticipating some time and point that integration going to take place,

319
00:24:23.833 --> 00:24:29.662
which we didn't know about, because that's what I got caught up in.

320
00:24:29.662 --> 00:24:36.836
But anyway, when they found out how good an athlete I was, they didn't bother me.

321
00:24:36.836 --> 00:24:39.527
I ride the bus every day.

322
00:24:39.527 --> 00:24:42.665
Or catch a ride, nobody bother me.

323
00:24:42.665 --> 00:24:47.149
But see we . . . They had a city bus in Mossville.

324
00:24:47.149 --> 00:24:52.082
That bus ran I think six or seven days a week.

325
00:24:52.082 --> 00:24:57.911
It was six o'clock, ten o'clock, three o'clock, and ten o'clock at night.

326
00:24:57.911 --> 00:25:03.740
We would pay ten cents because we were students to ride that bus.

327
00:25:03.740 --> 00:25:06.879
It passed right by Westlake High School.

328
00:25:06.879 --> 00:25:10.466
That’s how I got my high school education.

329
00:25:10.466 --> 00:25:17.192
So the same track coach that was watching me when I’d run in high school,

330
00:25:17.192 --> 00:25:24.367
when I got a scholarship to Grambling University, it was Grambling College then, to run track.

331
00:25:24.367 --> 00:25:34.680
The first year I ran track at Grambling I did so well, the second year that's when the schools integrated, the college integrated.

332
00:25:34.680 --> 00:25:37.370
When he found that out he

333
00:25:37.370 --> 00:25:43.648
<c.p>recruited me back to Lake Charles see, to run track at McNeese [State University].

334
00:25:43.648 --> 00:25:51.719
So that's where I wind up being at McNeese and I got all my three degrees at McNeese.

335
00:25:51.719 --> 00:25:54.858
My bachelor’s, my master's, and plus thirty.

336
00:25:54.858 --> 00:25:57.996
So that's the background on my education.

337
00:25:57.996 --> 00:26:06.516
That same school that had to pass me, I had to pass by, I wind up working at it.

338
00:26:06.516 --> 00:26:09.655
At Westlake High School coaching and teaching.

339
00:26:09.655 --> 00:26:11.000
So that's . . .

340
00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:17.069
<v ARSENEAULT>How far away from . . . How far did you have to commute to go to high school?

341
00:26:17.069 --> 00:26:18.586
<v WILLIAMS>It's about ten miles.

342
00:26:18.586 --> 00:26:22.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How far was it to the white school?

343
00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:23.010
<v WILLIAMS>About three.

344
00:26:23.010 --> 00:26:28.059
I could walk to Westlake High and back every day.

345
00:26:28.059 --> 00:26:31.088
<v ARSENEAULT>What year did it integrate here?

346
00:26:31.088 --> 00:26:35.632
<v WILLIAMS>Let's see that was in 1954, I believe . . . '56.

347
00:26:35.632 --> 00:26:39.167
I think it was somewhere in there.

348
00:26:39.167 --> 00:26:49.265
Because I went to the military two years in Korea, and then I came back and went back to school.

349
00:26:49.265 --> 00:26:52.799
But I can tell you some things.

350
00:26:52.799 --> 00:26:56.838
There was a senator, the name [William] Rainach . . .

351
00:26:56.838 --> 00:26:58.353
<v ARSENEAULT>What was the . . .?

352
00:26:58.353 --> 00:26:59.363
<v WILLIAMS>Senator Rainach.

353
00:26:59.363 --> 00:27:05.422
That's all the . . . You can find that in Baton Rouge, I'm sure.

354
00:27:05.422 --> 00:27:16.529
He was a state senator and he apparently passed a law after integration that no black could compete against whites in sports.

355
00:27:16.529 --> 00:27:27.637
So that kind of held things up when I was at McNeese and they were fixing to send us back to Grambling.

356
00:27:27.637 --> 00:27:40.260
But then we had people like Ms. [Combrey?] that was president of NAACP and she hauled us back and forth in New Orleans to court.

357
00:27:40.260 --> 00:27:43.794
So they finally wiped that law out.

358
00:27:43.794 --> 00:27:48.843
That's why you see black athletes competing at McNeese now.

359
00:27:48.843 --> 00:27:56.922
He didn't want . . . And that law stood for a little while but they ruled it unconstitutional.

360
00:27:56.922 --> 00:28:00.456
Well I got caught up in that.

361
00:28:00.456 --> 00:28:05.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about World War Two?

362
00:28:05.000 --> 00:28:13.200
<v WILLIAMS>The only thing I remember about World War Two is what I read, and I taught American history.

363
00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:14.567
<v ARSENEAULT>You weren't born . . .?

364
00:28:14.567 --> 00:28:19.122
<v WILLIAMS>I'm a Korean . . . My dad was in World War One.

365
00:28:19.122 --> 00:28:22.311
I can . . . I remember World War Two.

366
00:28:22.311 --> 00:28:26.411
I think I was in maybe high school, yeah.

367
00:28:26.411 --> 00:28:29.600
But I taught it in American history.

368
00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:38.256
But when I went to Westlake I found out from the other . . . All coaches had to teach social studies,

369
00:28:38.256 --> 00:28:44.633
and I was the only black coach and they had five other white coaches.

370
00:28:44.633 --> 00:28:53.289
The most important thing to them, and looked like the kids looked forward to it, was the Civil War.

371
00:28:53.289 --> 00:28:57.844
That was the great, and that's what they were teaching.

372
00:28:57.844 --> 00:29:04.678
Of course, I did a little bit different because I went back into slavery, see.

373
00:29:04.678 --> 00:29:08.778
I had a lot of white kids in there.

374
00:29:08.778 --> 00:29:16.067
<c.p>But I was telling . . . I want to share this with y'all and it's the God truth,

375
00:29:16.067 --> 00:29:21.533
because I told it in my church just a few days ago.

376
00:29:21.533 --> 00:29:28.367
We were talking about teaching and that's part of our Sunday school; teaching and praying.

377
00:29:28.367 --> 00:29:35.200
But I really never did have no fear when I went into white schools teaching.

378
00:29:35.200 --> 00:29:42.033
You had that stigma like they said that white kids were smarter than black kids.

379
00:29:42.033 --> 00:29:48.867
Some of them were saying smarter than . . . White teachers were smarter than the black teachers.

380
00:29:48.867 --> 00:29:53.878
I never did believe that, and I don't believe it today.

381
00:29:53.878 --> 00:29:57.978
So that's why when they integrated the schools here,

382
00:29:57.978 --> 00:30:06.178
they took the best . . . What they call the best black teachers and sent them to the white schools.

383
00:30:06.178 --> 00:30:12.556
What they call the weak teachers they left them in the predominantly black schools.

384
00:30:12.556 --> 00:30:18.933
Well, I was teaching American history and I’d go back to the Civil War.

385
00:30:18.933 --> 00:30:26.222
I had Doctor White, which is still living, he was the first medical doctor in Westlake.

386
00:30:26.222 --> 00:30:29.411
I had his son in that class.

387
00:30:29.411 --> 00:30:34.422
I had Doctor Caraway who runs the Caraway's Pharmacy in Westlake.

388
00:30:34.422 --> 00:30:38.067
I had his son and daughter in class.

389
00:30:38.067 --> 00:30:43.533
Then I had the principal, Vincent, had his daughter in my class.

390
00:30:43.533 --> 00:30:49.000
So I looked, I said, all these heavy people around here.

391
00:30:49.000 --> 00:30:54.517
Anyways, got on the . . . You ever seen the old chalkboards in school?

392
00:30:54.517 --> 00:31:00.034
They green and you take . . . Wash them and you have the eraser.

393
00:31:00.034 --> 00:31:07.391
Well, when all those kids got in my class I went and took all these dates,

394
00:31:07.391 --> 00:31:12.908
these events, and I put them on a line on the chalkboard.

395
00:31:12.908 --> 00:31:17.966
I had two chalkboards, because I never did teach sitting down.

396
00:31:17.966 --> 00:31:21.644
Chalkboard over here and a chalkboard over here.

397
00:31:21.644 --> 00:31:27.161
I put all the events on there, the dates and what happened.

398
00:31:27.161 --> 00:31:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a timeline?

399
00:31:29.000 --> 00:31:30.185
<v WILLIAMS>Like a timeline.

400
00:31:30.185 --> 00:31:33.345
Then I took an eraser and erased them.

401
00:31:33.345 --> 00:31:39.269
They couldn't see them, but I could see them because I put them up there.

402
00:31:39.269 --> 00:31:42.034
So when I got up and went

403
00:31:42.034 --> 00:31:48.353
<c.p>to teaching and when I got through rattling them dates and them events and what happened,

404
00:31:48.353 --> 00:31:53.487
they went back and told their parents, "Oh, Coach is a smart man.

405
00:31:53.487 --> 00:31:57.832
We all going to get in his class because he knows.”

406
00:31:57.832 --> 00:32:02.966
That's the way it happened and anybody can tell you even Hal McMillan.

407
00:32:02.966 --> 00:32:04.151
You know Hal?

408
00:32:04.151 --> 00:32:05.336
The police juror?

409
00:32:05.336 --> 00:32:06.521
I told him.

410
00:32:06.521 --> 00:32:11.655
That's I guess a mark for me as far as the relationship go.

411
00:32:11.655 --> 00:32:14.420
They say, “Get in Coach Williams’ class.”

412
00:32:14.420 --> 00:32:22.714
Then again it put me on the spot, because I had to really do some more teaching then, from then on.

413
00:32:22.714 --> 00:32:29.034
There were other areas you had to teach where you weren't dealing with events and dates.

414
00:32:29.034 --> 00:32:32.193
But they would follow me in the book.

415
00:32:32.193 --> 00:32:35.353
Say, "God dog, he right on all this!

416
00:32:35.353 --> 00:32:38.118
We looking at it in the book."

417
00:32:38.118 --> 00:32:41.672
And say, "He ain't using no paper or nothing."

418
00:32:41.672 --> 00:32:45.622
So it's all kinds of little tricks out there now.

419
00:32:45.622 --> 00:32:56.286
Even do it in coaching a lot of time just not on that . . . I guess I was blessed with that because I don't think I'm that smart,

420
00:32:56.286 --> 00:32:58.655
but I got away with it.

421
00:32:58.655 --> 00:33:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any of your favorite teachers in Mossville?

422
00:33:03.000 --> 00:33:05.028
<v WILLIAMS>Oh yeah. [Zora Lyons?], [P.

423
00:33:05.028 --> 00:33:08.680
M. Washington?], H. T. Williams, which was the principal.

424
00:33:08.680 --> 00:33:13.142
Most of those were the only teachers I had in Mossville.

425
00:33:13.142 --> 00:33:16.388
All the other teachers were from Lake Charles.

426
00:33:16.388 --> 00:33:23.690
But those were . . . And Ms. Lyons taught me something that I'll never forget and I couldn't understand it.

427
00:33:23.690 --> 00:33:27.747
She was considered to be a mean teacher, but strict.

428
00:33:27.747 --> 00:33:31.804
The bell rang one day, and we had these desks.

429
00:33:31.804 --> 00:33:37.889
We had . . . . What do you call it up on that . . . And they had the rows,

430
00:33:37.889 --> 00:33:42.351
so it was some books this girl left on the floor.

431
00:33:42.351 --> 00:33:43.163
Some magazines.

432
00:33:43.163 --> 00:33:52.088
So everybody getting up and I'm being the last one she said, "Look, pick up those books and put them in place."

433
00:33:52.088 --> 00:33:56.956
I made the mistake, I [. . .?] I said, “I didn't put them there.”

434
00:33:56.956 --> 00:33:59.390
What did I do that for?

435
00:33:59.390 --> 00:34:02.230
I got one of the worst whippings.

436
00:34:02.230 --> 00:34:07.098
Man it took me to understand later she didn’t care who put

437
00:34:07.098 --> 00:34:11.155
<c.p>them there; she gave me an order to move them.

438
00:34:11.155 --> 00:34:15.618
And that stuck with me today and I can see why.

439
00:34:15.618 --> 00:34:18.052
But I thought she was mean.

440
00:34:18.052 --> 00:34:20.891
I said, “She was teaching me what?”

441
00:34:20.891 --> 00:34:24.948
Look, I you give me an order, you do it.

442
00:34:24.948 --> 00:34:27.382
And I feel that way today.

443
00:34:27.382 --> 00:34:34.685
They tried to get me . . . I went up to Philadelphia to visit after I won the state championship,

444
00:34:34.685 --> 00:34:37.525
my team won in all three sports.

445
00:34:37.525 --> 00:34:44.016
I went up there to visit my mama . . . My biological mama, and my sister, and brother.

446
00:34:44.016 --> 00:34:48.884
One of their best friends to my sister was a school teacher.

447
00:34:48.884 --> 00:34:52.941
Well they’d heard about me and they saw my clippings.

448
00:34:52.941 --> 00:34:54.563
They lived in Germantown.

449
00:34:54.563 --> 00:34:58.214
I don't know if you know anything about Pennsylvania.

450
00:34:58.214 --> 00:35:00.243
Germantown, that's the east side.

451
00:35:00.243 --> 00:35:06.734
But where my mother lived in Germantown, about three blocks down they had Germantown High School.

452
00:35:06.734 --> 00:35:07.545
Big school.

453
00:35:07.545 --> 00:35:13.225
They had so many students in that school they had to come in shifts.

454
00:35:13.225 --> 00:35:17.282
Some come in the morning, some come in the afternoon.

455
00:35:17.282 --> 00:35:22.556
But they had a football . . . so they told . . . I was up there visiting,

456
00:35:22.556 --> 00:35:28.641
they say, “We want you to come meet our principal and walk over our school.”

457
00:35:28.641 --> 00:35:33.103
So I went and he took me all over the school.

458
00:35:33.103 --> 00:35:36.755
He was an Italian guy, almost German, I guess.

459
00:35:36.755 --> 00:35:40.000
And we talked and he showed me.

460
00:35:40.000 --> 00:35:43.568
But I observed some things that I couldn't agree with.

461
00:35:43.568 --> 00:35:47.492
But he wanted to give me a job up there coaching.

462
00:35:47.492 --> 00:35:53.558
But I . . . The disciplines I noticed in the classroom, the kids did what they wanted to do.

463
00:35:53.558 --> 00:35:57.482
If they didn't want to learn, they put they heads down.

464
00:35:57.482 --> 00:35:59.266
The teacher didn't bother them.

465
00:35:59.266 --> 00:36:02.121
They walk out the classroom, they get ready.

466
00:36:02.121 --> 00:36:09.256
And if they want to play a game while she was teaching, or he was teaching, they didn't bother them.

467
00:36:09.256 --> 00:36:10.683
I said, no way.

468
00:36:10.683 --> 00:36:13.181
I can't have this kind of discipline.

469
00:36:13.181 --> 00:36:16.035
If I'm teaching, you going to pay attention.

470
00:36:16.035 --> 00:36:17.462
You going to listen.

471
00:36:17.462 --> 00:36:19.603
I'm going to get your attention.

472
00:36:19.603 --> 00:36:24.955
You not going to go to sleep, and you’re not going to do anything else.

473
00:36:24.955 --> 00:36:28.879
So that's the reason I didn't wind up with that job.

474
00:36:28.879 --> 00:36:34.945
I guess I'm glad I didn't, because I don't think I would have lasted under those conditions.

475
00:36:34.945 --> 00:36:36.729
I couldn't conform to that.

476
00:36:36.729 --> 00:36:38.869
I can't conform to it today.

477
00:36:38.869 --> 00:36:41.010
<c.p>I'm going to have some discipline.

478
00:36:41.010 --> 00:36:45.291
I guess that's why the good lord got me out in time.

479
00:36:45.291 --> 00:36:51.000
Because they would have fired me with the way some of the kids the way . . .

480
00:36:51.000 --> 00:36:55.516
<v ARSENEAULT>When you were younger, where did the young folks gather?

481
00:36:55.516 --> 00:36:58.677
What did they do for fun here?

482
00:36:58.677 --> 00:36:59.581
<v WILLIAMS>In Mossville?

483
00:36:59.581 --> 00:37:04.548
On Sundays I tell you we were out there playing softball.

484
00:37:04.548 --> 00:37:09.968
Now on this side of Mossville . . . They had two sides of Mossville.

485
00:37:09.968 --> 00:37:16.290
They had the north side, I guess you called, and maybe the south side.

486
00:37:16.290 --> 00:37:19.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was different about them?

487
00:37:19.000 --> 00:37:20.287
<v WILLIAMS>Well, the families.

488
00:37:20.287 --> 00:37:25.865
Like on the what you call ‘em, everybody was kin on that side.

489
00:37:25.865 --> 00:37:35.304
We’d go up there, and like I'm telling you in Saprack, on Sundays after church and we'd have a big softball game.

490
00:37:35.304 --> 00:37:37.449
We’d play against each other.

491
00:37:37.449 --> 00:37:42.169
Now let me tell you what the grown folks were doing.

492
00:37:42.169 --> 00:37:50.321
The grown folk were back in the woods, and they were barbequing beef, pork, and they also were gambling.

493
00:37:50.321 --> 00:37:53.753
They had gambling tables back in the woods.

494
00:37:53.753 --> 00:37:56.328
The gamblers, the men, not us.

495
00:37:56.328 --> 00:38:01.047
So these were recreation activities with them when we play ball.

496
00:38:01.047 --> 00:38:07.912
Then they had a little canteen they called Sweet Dreams right down there in the curve.

497
00:38:07.912 --> 00:38:12.632
That was a canteen there that ice cream and cold drinks.

498
00:38:12.632 --> 00:38:19.926
And they had a couple of slot machines like the slot machines you see in the casinos.

499
00:38:19.926 --> 00:38:21.642
They were in Mossville.

500
00:38:21.642 --> 00:38:27.649
The grocery store had them, the canteen had them, and we would play them.

501
00:38:27.649 --> 00:38:30.223
They ain't worried about nobody's age.

502
00:38:30.223 --> 00:38:34.514
You could win all of the nickels and the pennies.

503
00:38:34.514 --> 00:38:37.088
That's where the girls would gather.

504
00:38:37.088 --> 00:38:37.517
That's

505
00:38:37.517 --> 00:38:38.804
<c.p>where everybody gathered.

506
00:38:38.804 --> 00:38:41.378
Have fun listening at the jukebox.

507
00:38:41.378 --> 00:38:50.818
So that was our main source of . . . On Sunday evenings this road would be full of kids walking going to the canteen.

508
00:38:50.818 --> 00:38:53.821
They'd have something going on up there.

509
00:38:53.821 --> 00:38:55.537
That was our recreation.

510
00:38:55.537 --> 00:39:01.544
Like this? [Referring to the Rigmaiden Recreation Center] We ain’t have nothing like this.

511
00:39:01.544 --> 00:39:09.267
Only recreation take place had to be at your house in a family yard where they playing Indians,

512
00:39:09.267 --> 00:39:18.706
or we made bow and arrows out of wood, sharpen them down, I don't know how some of us didn't get hurt.

513
00:39:18.706 --> 00:39:23.426
We had bow and arrows made out of these rattan vines.

514
00:39:23.426 --> 00:39:26.000
That's about it for recreation.

515
00:39:26.000 --> 00:39:31.250
We didn't have no . . . Only swimming you got to go in the dynamite hole.

516
00:39:31.250 --> 00:39:38.000
My daddy whipped me one time, and now my mama whipped me . . . I can go kiss her grave,

517
00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:42.500
if I had a dollar the times she put on my butt.

518
00:39:42.500 --> 00:39:45.125
But he whipped me only one time.

519
00:39:45.125 --> 00:39:47.000
He'd always talk to me.

520
00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:50.750
He told me, "Boy don't go in the dynamite hole."

521
00:39:50.750 --> 00:39:55.250
Well the dynamite hole was over there by . . . Almost to Highway 90.

522
00:39:55.250 --> 00:39:57.125
We walked through the woods.

523
00:39:57.125 --> 00:39:59.750
But all my buddies, they was swimming.

524
00:39:59.750 --> 00:40:01.625
I'm sitting on the bank.

525
00:40:01.625 --> 00:40:04.625
So I decide I'm going to get in.

526
00:40:04.625 --> 00:40:13.625
I couldn't swim that good, but I got in the shallow parts and that's where I learn how to swim in the dynamite hole.

527
00:40:13.625 --> 00:40:21.125
But I came home that evening and [. . .?] he could look at my head and tell that I'd been in swimming.

528
00:40:21.125 --> 00:40:23.750
That's the worst whipping he gave me.

529
00:40:23.750 --> 00:40:33.125
I thought I could get by and not knowing that he could look at my head, look at the hair, and tell I'd been swimming.

530
00:40:33.125 --> 00:40:38.000
So that's the only place we could swim was in the dynamite hole.

531
00:40:38.000 --> 00:40:40.250
Complete with turtles, snakes, and leech.

532
00:40:40.250 --> 00:40:42.875
You don't know what a leech is.

533
00:40:42.875 --> 00:40:45.125
You know what a leech is?

534
00:40:45.125 --> 00:40:47.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Sticks to your body?

535
00:40:47.000 --> 00:40:50.261
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah, I had a bunch of them on me.

536
00:40:50.261 --> 00:40:52.435
They like to get around your

537
00:40:52.435 --> 00:40:55.333
<c.p>feet between your toes and suck your blood.

538
00:40:55.333 --> 00:40:56.783
We survived all that.

539
00:40:56.783 --> 00:40:59.681
ARSENEAULT: Alright, start kind of wrapping it up.

540
00:40:59.681 --> 00:41:02.217
I have like the last few questions.

541
00:41:02.217 --> 00:41:04.754
So what does home mean to you?

542
00:41:04.754 --> 00:41:05.116
<v WILLIAMS>Home?

543
00:41:05.116 --> 00:41:08.014
Well, home means everything. [We’re nothing without home?].

544
00:41:08.014 --> 00:41:09.826
My whole life was home.

545
00:41:09.826 --> 00:41:15.623
If you go up and stay the night some people, some elders lived in Beaumont [Texas].

546
00:41:15.623 --> 00:41:20.333
And during the summer we would go over there and stay with them.

547
00:41:20.333 --> 00:41:25.406
My auntie, and vice versa, their kids would come stay with us one summer.

548
00:41:25.406 --> 00:41:33.377
But I'd always, I’d get to Beaumont, stay over there about three or four days, and I'm ready to come back home.

549
00:41:33.377 --> 00:41:36.275
In the same way, I’d go to Pennsylvania.

550
00:41:36.275 --> 00:41:42.435
When I go and stay one week, they go up there, want us to stay a month.

551
00:41:42.435 --> 00:41:47.145
I might stay but one week up, I'm ready to come back home.

552
00:41:47.145 --> 00:41:51.130
That big city life just never was a thing for me.

553
00:41:51.130 --> 00:41:57.290
I can adjust to it, but I just . . . And today I can't live in the big city.

554
00:41:57.290 --> 00:42:04.174
I'm trying to figure out now where if I have to move, but it won't be in no city.

555
00:42:04.174 --> 00:42:08.522
I have nothing against it, but I’m just not the city type.

556
00:42:08.522 --> 00:42:14.319
When I built this house up here and I said, “We going to build a house.”

557
00:42:14.319 --> 00:42:20.841
Matter of fact I got that plan from up in Philadelphia, the house that I’m in in now.

558
00:42:20.841 --> 00:42:27.362
I said, “I'm going to get me another land and put it right in the middle of it.”

559
00:42:27.362 --> 00:42:30.623
And that's what I did, I bought three acres.

560
00:42:30.623 --> 00:42:36.058
I didn’t put it in the middle but I put it at the highest point,

561
00:42:36.058 --> 00:42:42.580
which I got two acres on this side of me and then a half on the other side.

562
00:42:42.580 --> 00:42:48.739
But I didn't have no neighbors but one that . . . When I built it and that's the Mosses,

563
00:42:48.739 --> 00:42:52.000
my auntie, which I bought out of this subdivision.

564
00:42:52.000 --> 00:42:55.986
But now they got four or five families around here now.

565
00:42:55.986 --> 00:42:59.609
But we all get along because we know each other.

566
00:42:59.609 --> 00:43:04.319
But when I get ready to get up and walk out at night,

567
00:43:04.319 --> 00:43:10.841
walk out in the street, walk around my house with half of my clothes on, I feel comfortable.

568
00:43:10.841 --> 00:43:13.377
You can't do that in the city.

569
00:43:13.377 --> 00:43:16.275
I sit out on the porch in my

570
00:43:16.275 --> 00:43:17.000
<c.p>pajamas.

571
00:43:17.000 --> 00:43:19.548
Home was just about everything.

572
00:43:19.548 --> 00:43:23.625
There were three things in your life then.

573
00:43:23.625 --> 00:43:26.683
It was home, church, and school.

574
00:43:26.683 --> 00:43:31.269
We didn't have no TV, everybody had a radio.

575
00:43:31.269 --> 00:43:40.442
Matter of fact, when I . . . The first TV I saw, I think it was in Pennsylvania up there.

576
00:43:40.442 --> 00:43:48.087
They got all these buildings up there, they build three stories, three or four stories,

577
00:43:48.087 --> 00:43:53.692
so for a while a room . . . Them houses this close together,

578
00:43:53.692 --> 00:44:03.885
you look into the other people, you couldn't hear but you could see the picture of what was going on.

579
00:44:03.885 --> 00:44:10.000
You couldn't hear, but you would watch the other people. [Laughs]

580
00:44:10.000 --> 00:44:12.979
<v ARSENEAULT>You’d be watching TV through their window?

581
00:44:12.979 --> 00:44:13.404
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah.

582
00:44:13.404 --> 00:44:19.362
You couldn't hear what they was saying, but you could see what was happening.

583
00:44:19.362 --> 00:44:22.766
We had a theater one time in Westlake.

584
00:44:22.766 --> 00:44:24.043
A movie theater.

585
00:44:24.043 --> 00:44:31.702
It was segregated, because white people go on the bottom and we had to go up the stairs.

586
00:44:31.702 --> 00:44:41.489
It was a nice what you call it, but you couldn’t go in the same . . . They had two different windows that you paid.

587
00:44:41.489 --> 00:44:43.191
I seen a lot.

588
00:44:43.191 --> 00:44:43.617
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

589
00:44:43.617 --> 00:44:46.170
<v WILLIAMS>That was part of our recreation,

590
00:44:46.170 --> 00:44:54.681
too because you see on Saturdays . . . We couldn’t wait until Saturday because that's when the cowboy movies in the continuous.

591
00:44:54.681 --> 00:44:58.511
Never hardly we’d go to the movie on Sundays.

592
00:44:58.511 --> 00:45:07.872
Mostly every Saturday, we were going to head to. . . It's going to be Gene Autry or Roy Rogers, or Wild Bill Hickok.

593
00:45:07.872 --> 00:45:12.553
<v ARSENEAULT>Kind of going off that, what does community mean to you?

594
00:45:12.553 --> 00:45:14.255
<v WILLIAMS>Community means a lot.

595
00:45:14.255 --> 00:45:15.957
That's all I know.

596
00:45:15.957 --> 00:45:18.511
All my background in this community.

597
00:45:18.511 --> 00:45:23.617
Like I said, everybody knew everybody; everybody tried to help everybody out.

598
00:45:23.617 --> 00:45:25.745
They share with one another.

599
00:45:25.745 --> 00:45:28.723
If somebody was sick, everybody knew it.

600
00:45:28.723 --> 00:45:35.532
They'd go over there and sometimes they sit up all night with people who was sick.

601
00:45:35.532 --> 00:45:41.915
Just visiting, make a big bonfire in the yard, and we’d sit around the bonfire.

602
00:45:41.915 --> 00:45:46.596
That would happen sometimes all night until the person got well.

603
00:45:46.596 --> 00:45:48.723
That’s the way people did.

604
00:45:48.723 --> 00:45:53.830
If somebody passed, everybody went to their family to share their grief.

605
00:45:53.830 --> 00:45:55.957
So community meant a lot.

606
00:45:55.957 --> 00:46:00.213
I saw so much changes in the community over there.

607
00:46:00.213 --> 00:46:02.766
Of course you can't stop change.

608
00:46:02.766 --> 00:46:05.745
Change got to take its course somewhere.

609
00:46:05.745 --> 00:46:08.298
But community taught me a lot.

610
00:46:08.298 --> 00:46:17.234
Of course, somebody, I tell you walking down the street and you do something bad, they going to correct your butt.

611
00:46:17.234 --> 00:46:22.766
And you get home, and then find you’re going to get some more.

612
00:46:22.766 --> 00:46:25.319
Now today it's just the opposite.

613
00:46:25.319 --> 00:46:32.553
You go out there and correct somebody's child, they going to come and want to shoot you.

614
00:46:32.553 --> 00:46:35.106
Don’t want you to tell them.

615
00:46:35.106 --> 00:46:37.660
That wasn't the case back there.

616
00:46:37.660 --> 00:46:41.915
You wouldn't dare do something like that and sassy somebody.

617
00:46:41.915 --> 00:46:49.149
They going to whip your butt, and when you get home you going to get some more.

618
00:46:49.149 --> 00:46:56.383
So that shows you some ties that kind of protected your behavior in a lot of cases.

619
00:46:56.383 --> 00:47:05.319
Be careful, you think about what you're going to do before you do it, because they’re going to be some consequences.

620
00:47:05.319 --> 00:47:10.000
It ain't going to be long before they know it.

621
00:47:10.000 --> 00:47:13.357
I'm going to tell you this right quick.

622
00:47:13.357 --> 00:47:16.294
One of the worse whippings we gotten.

623
00:47:16.294 --> 00:47:21.748
Me and my little buddies had some devil in us, my little friends.

624
00:47:21.748 --> 00:47:25.944
The same guy told me about the alligator down there?

625
00:47:25.944 --> 00:47:28.462
He raised a bunch of chickens.

626
00:47:28.462 --> 00:47:31.818
Not only was he in that business and

627
00:47:31.818 --> 00:47:35.175
<c.p>grocery business, he was in the muskrat business.

628
00:47:35.175 --> 00:47:37.692
He trapped muskrats, and coons [raccoons].

629
00:47:37.692 --> 00:47:43.986
He had big old lines in the back of his house where he skin them,

630
00:47:43.986 --> 00:47:50.280
and he dry that out and take them to the market and he'd sell them.

631
00:47:50.280 --> 00:47:55.734
They say they make, whoever make purses and all that out of them.

632
00:47:55.734 --> 00:47:59.510
Anyway, he had chickens running all over the place.

633
00:47:59.510 --> 00:48:02.028
So we all had BB guns.

634
00:48:02.028 --> 00:48:06.643
And we . . . Down the railroad track, you see right down there?

635
00:48:06.643 --> 00:48:10.000
That was two big old rice farms.

636
00:48:10.000 --> 00:48:10.382
<v ARSENEAULT>Rice?

637
00:48:10.382 --> 00:48:11.145
<v WILLIAMS>Rice farms.

638
00:48:11.145 --> 00:48:12.671
Where they grow rice.

639
00:48:12.671 --> 00:48:16.868
See, they had some rice farms out here and cotton farms.

640
00:48:16.868 --> 00:48:22.209
We go down there with our BB guns trying to shoot these marsh hens.

641
00:48:22.209 --> 00:48:24.117
Marsh hens is a bird.

642
00:48:24.117 --> 00:48:26.025
So we could eat them.

643
00:48:26.025 --> 00:48:33.656
We wasn't getting too successful, so we decided one day that we was going to shoot some of his chickens.

644
00:48:33.656 --> 00:48:35.945
They were running in the woods.

645
00:48:35.945 --> 00:48:42.813
So we did that, went back to his sister's house which was . . . her son . . . We was all friends.

646
00:48:42.813 --> 00:48:47.773
We dug us a whole in the ground, made us a barbeque pit.

647
00:48:47.773 --> 00:48:50.825
So we was going to barbeque these birds.

648
00:48:50.825 --> 00:48:58.075
What we did to kind of disguise them, we skin them so you couldn't tell it was a chicken.

649
00:48:58.075 --> 00:49:01.127
We thought we were getting away with it.

650
00:49:01.127 --> 00:49:02.653
We were having fun.

651
00:49:02.653 --> 00:49:07.995
We barbequed and ate, but he went and told it when he got home.

652
00:49:07.995 --> 00:49:14.100
When I got home I didn't get a good whipping like I'd been with that switch.

653
00:49:14.100 --> 00:49:18.678
My mama, we got a big old tree in the front yard.

654
00:49:18.678 --> 00:49:23.638
It was so big that's where they parked their cars as a garage.

655
00:49:23.638 --> 00:49:28.217
She got the Bible and she went to the book of Proverbs.

656
00:49:28.217 --> 00:49:31.651
And she sit down with me under that tree.

657
00:49:31.651 --> 00:49:37.756
And when she went in the book of Proverbs, I say, well, this the worst whipping.

658
00:49:37.756 --> 00:49:39.663
And I still say that.

659
00:49:39.663 --> 00:49:42.334
That's the worst whipping I ever had.

660
00:49:42.334 --> 00:49:44.623
Because she worked on my mind.

661
00:49:44.623 --> 00:49:53.017
I remember because my daddy always said, "Boy, you go out here and get in trouble and go to jail, I'm going

662
00:49:53.017 --> 00:49:54.925
<c.p>to come see about you.

663
00:49:54.925 --> 00:49:57.978
But if you steal something, I ain't coming.

664
00:49:57.978 --> 00:49:59.504
You going to stay."

665
00:49:59.504 --> 00:50:00.648
He meant that.

666
00:50:00.648 --> 00:50:06.372
“Anything else I'll come see about you, but if you steal something, I ain't coming.”

667
00:50:06.372 --> 00:50:07.516
He meant that.

668
00:50:07.516 --> 00:50:09.424
I tell my kids that.

669
00:50:09.424 --> 00:50:12.095
I tell even my football players that.

670
00:50:12.095 --> 00:50:22.015
Butch can tell you, because if you steal something I don't care how small it is, you going to pay for it four or five times.

671
00:50:22.015 --> 00:50:23.923
It's going to come back.

672
00:50:23.923 --> 00:50:28.501
And while I'm on that note, I'm going to share something else.

673
00:50:28.501 --> 00:50:30.791
You ever heard of Cagle Brothers?

674
00:50:30.791 --> 00:50:31.554
<v SHEARMAN>Spell it?

675
00:50:31.554 --> 00:50:32.317
<v WILLIAMS>Cagle Brothers.

676
00:50:32.317 --> 00:50:33.080
<v SHEARMAN>Oh, C-A-G-L-E?

677
00:50:33.080 --> 00:50:34.606
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah the Cagle Brothers.

678
00:50:34.606 --> 00:50:35.369
Car dealers.

679
00:50:35.369 --> 00:50:36.132
<v SHEARMAN>Yes, sir.

680
00:50:36.132 --> 00:50:38.040
Bob, and Robin, and Joe.

681
00:50:38.040 --> 00:50:41.855
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah, you know they had the dealership on Broad Street.

682
00:50:41.855 --> 00:50:43.000
<v SHEARMAN>Yes, sir.

683
00:50:43.000 --> 00:50:46.205
<v WILLIAMS>My mama used to work for them.

684
00:50:46.205 --> 00:50:50.784
So I went and bought a brand new Savoy Plymouth.

685
00:50:50.784 --> 00:50:51.700
Brand new.

686
00:50:51.700 --> 00:50:56.278
That was the first brand new car I'd ever owned.

687
00:50:56.278 --> 00:50:57.194
I was

688
00:50:57.194 --> 00:50:59.484
<c.p>working at this recreation center.

689
00:50:59.484 --> 00:51:08.183
Matter of fact, I happened to develop all this around here, come under my . . . Even that gym over there.

690
00:51:08.183 --> 00:51:15.051
I'm the one drew up the . . . I was getting a school check which today now,

691
00:51:15.051 --> 00:51:19.630
look, me and my wife been married for sixty-three years.

692
00:51:19.630 --> 00:51:22.835
Now you might call me a chump.

693
00:51:22.835 --> 00:51:25.582
I don't care what you say.

694
00:51:25.582 --> 00:51:30.619
Up until this day I have never handled my school check.

695
00:51:30.619 --> 00:51:32.451
She always get it.

696
00:51:32.451 --> 00:51:35.656
But see, I had a recreation check.

697
00:51:35.656 --> 00:51:37.029
That was mine.

698
00:51:37.029 --> 00:51:41.608
That check come to me; it came twice a month.

699
00:51:41.608 --> 00:51:46.187
But the school check, now I haven't touched it today.

700
00:51:46.187 --> 00:51:48.476
She handles all of that.

701
00:51:48.476 --> 00:51:51.223
So anyway, I went to Westlake.

702
00:51:51.223 --> 00:51:52.597
Broussard’s Grocery Store.

703
00:51:52.597 --> 00:51:56.260
That was the main grocery store in Westlake.

704
00:51:56.260 --> 00:51:57.176
Meat market.

705
00:51:57.176 --> 00:51:59.007
Good friend of mine.

706
00:51:59.007 --> 00:52:09.081
Well, he had this white cashier lady, husband who lived in the back of the store that flew these confederate signs . . . Flags.

707
00:52:09.081 --> 00:52:15.949
So we always . . . About all them confederate flags, say, “Well they don't like black people.”

708
00:52:15.949 --> 00:52:18.696
Well, she worked in that store.

709
00:52:18.696 --> 00:52:27.396
One of the Broussard’s, I guess main cashiers . . . But anyway I went in that day to cash my check,

710
00:52:27.396 --> 00:52:30.601
had a lot of people in there.

711
00:52:30.601 --> 00:52:33.348
I purchased some meats, and steak.

712
00:52:33.348 --> 00:52:36.095
She cashed my check as usual.

713
00:52:36.095 --> 00:52:38.385
She gave me my change.

714
00:52:38.385 --> 00:52:42.048
I’m looking at her when she counted it.

715
00:52:42.048 --> 00:52:48.000
I said to myself, that lady giving me too much money back.

716
00:52:48.000 --> 00:52:51.234
But they had a lot of people in there.

717
00:52:51.234 --> 00:52:54.827
I’ll show you how the devil work on your mind.

718
00:52:54.827 --> 00:53:00.577
My good conscience say, this is an opportunity to show this lady how honest you is.

719
00:53:00.577 --> 00:53:04.530
Let her know that she has given you too much money.

720
00:53:04.530 --> 00:53:10.279
Well the old devil say then, no, keep it, because she don't like you no way.

721
00:53:10.279 --> 00:53:12.794
So that's what I decided to do.

722
00:53:12.794 --> 00:53:16.028
I goes out, get in my car, counts it.

723
00:53:16.028 --> 00:53:19.622
It’s like the lady give me twenty-something dollars too much.

724
00:53:19.622 --> 00:53:21.418
Again, something say go back.

725
00:53:21.418 --> 00:53:22.496
No, keep it.

726
00:53:22.496 --> 00:53:28.965
So I was scheduled that night to go and scout a game in Grand Avenue in DeQuincy [Louisiana].

727
00:53:28.965 --> 00:53:30.761
That was one our competitors.

728
00:53:30.761 --> 00:53:32.558
They was on Grand Avenue.

729
00:53:32.558 --> 00:53:37.948
I had to go scout them because we were going to play them the next

730
00:53:37.948 --> 00:53:38.307
<c.p>week.

731
00:53:38.307 --> 00:53:45.135
I said, “Well, I'm going to use this twenty bucks up there and have a little fun with it.”

732
00:53:45.135 --> 00:53:46.213
This twenty-something dollars.

733
00:53:46.213 --> 00:53:49.806
So I goes on up there and scout the game.

734
00:53:49.806 --> 00:53:57.712
And then normally, when I'm on my way home, I stop by the beer joint and had a few beers with it.

735
00:53:57.712 --> 00:53:59.149
Got in my car.

736
00:53:59.149 --> 00:54:04.539
On the way home, about halfway between here and DeQuincy, my water pump went out.

737
00:54:04.539 --> 00:54:06.695
Now that's a brand new car.

738
00:54:06.695 --> 00:54:12.444
Water pump . . . I had to get a wrecker . . . pay a wrecker that tow my car home.

739
00:54:12.444 --> 00:54:17.835
I believe the honor of the story is because of my dishonesty, God punished me.

740
00:54:17.835 --> 00:54:23.943
Because there ain't no way no brand new car blows a water pump supposed to go out.

741
00:54:23.943 --> 00:54:26.818
Whether it's true or not, I believe that.

742
00:54:26.818 --> 00:54:33.645
If I'd of gave that money back to that lady I don't believe my car would have broke down.

743
00:54:33.645 --> 00:54:36.520
So I ain't superstitious, but I believe that.

744
00:54:36.520 --> 00:54:42.270
So like I say, if you take something from somebody, you going to pay for it.

745
00:54:42.270 --> 00:54:43.348
If you're dishonest.

746
00:54:43.348 --> 00:54:52.331
I think that may be one of my greatest assets in growing up and teaching is always try to be honest and fair to everybody.

747
00:54:52.331 --> 00:54:54.846
I don't care what color you are.

748
00:54:54.846 --> 00:54:58.080
That's what they found out about me in Westlake.

749
00:54:58.080 --> 00:55:00.236
This man don't make no difference.

750
00:55:00.236 --> 00:55:02.392
I'll tell you something, ask Butch.

751
00:55:02.392 --> 00:55:04.908
I kept a paddle everywhere I go.

752
00:55:04.908 --> 00:55:07.783
With my team . . . Kept it in my gym.

753
00:55:07.783 --> 00:55:13.532
The teachers or the principal got ready to discipline somebody they say, “Send him to coach.”

754
00:55:13.532 --> 00:55:18.922
They don't want to come to me because they know they going to get it.

755
00:55:18.922 --> 00:55:20.000
Ask him.

756
00:55:20.000 --> 00:55:24.359
So when the schools integrated, they say, “Oh, no, there’s another one.

757
00:55:24.359 --> 00:55:25.085
Ask him.

758
00:55:25.085 --> 00:55:30.897
Oh no, he ain’t going to go down there with them white boys with that paddle.”

759
00:55:30.897 --> 00:55:33.077
I took my paddle down there,

760
00:55:33.077 --> 00:55:41.432
and put it on . . . I got some white boys today who will tell you that they proud of the way I handle them,

761
00:55:41.432 --> 00:55:43.248
and they all good businessmen.

762
00:55:43.248 --> 00:55:44.701
Boy run Silver Constructions.

763
00:55:44.701 --> 00:55:45.427
Eddie Prewit.

764
00:55:45.427 --> 00:55:46.154
That’s Eddie.

765
00:55:46.154 --> 00:55:51.603
Eddie brag on me say, “You put that paddle on my butt so many times.”

766
00:55:51.603 --> 00:55:56.688
But they found out then, they thought I was going to make a difference.

767
00:55:56.688 --> 00:55:58.504
It don't make no difference.

768
00:55:58.504 --> 00:55:58.868
You

769
00:55:58.868 --> 00:56:00.684
<c.p>teaching kids, you teaching them.

770
00:56:00.684 --> 00:56:02.137
You discipline, you discipline.

771
00:56:02.137 --> 00:56:04.316
Don't care what color they are.

772
00:56:04.316 --> 00:56:07.949
So I think that probably . . . And that wasn't my intentions.

773
00:56:07.949 --> 00:56:10.855
I just wanted to do the right thing.

774
00:56:10.855 --> 00:56:12.308
I'm still that way.

775
00:56:12.308 --> 00:56:18.846
You can go to prison and them guys going to tell you at the prison, white and black.

776
00:56:18.846 --> 00:56:24.295
I'm going to be fair and straight with you as long as you don't clown.

777
00:56:24.295 --> 00:56:29.017
I'm going to treat you with respect, and I'm going to demand respect.

778
00:56:29.017 --> 00:56:30.833
I think they do respect.

779
00:56:30.833 --> 00:56:33.376
Even though they locked up up there.

780
00:56:33.376 --> 00:56:37.009
I didn’t lock them up, the juries locked them up.

781
00:56:37.009 --> 00:56:41.731
So it’s my job to try and help them, not to punish them.

782
00:56:41.731 --> 00:56:45.000
So I could talk for days, I guess.

783
00:56:45.000 --> 00:56:50.600
<v ARSENEAULT>Well, we definitely want to sit down and do a longer interview with you.

784
00:56:50.600 --> 00:56:52.600
I had one final question.

785
00:56:52.600 --> 00:57:00.200
What's the most important thing that you want people to remember about Mossville that's not in the history books?

786
00:57:00.200 --> 00:57:07.000
<v WILLIAMS>I want them to remember the churches, the schools, the recreation, the political ties that we had.

787
00:57:07.000 --> 00:57:11.400
Because one time Mossville had six hundred and some registered voters.

788
00:57:11.400 --> 00:57:13.400
You know what they did?

789
00:57:13.400 --> 00:57:15.000
They just like ducks.

790
00:57:15.000 --> 00:57:17.000
One duck follow the other.

791
00:57:17.000 --> 00:57:17.800
Like geese.

792
00:57:17.800 --> 00:57:23.800
You had the leaders in the community say, “We’re going to vote for this man,

793
00:57:23.800 --> 00:57:29.400
we’re going to vote for this sheriff, we’re going to vote for this reason."

794
00:57:29.400 --> 00:57:30.600
Everybody block vote.

795
00:57:30.600 --> 00:57:41.000
That's why a lot of the politicians gave a lot of respect to Mossville, because they know them people going to vote the same way.

796
00:57:41.000 --> 00:57:46.230
<v ARSENEAULT>So you influence the leaders, and then the leaders can influence the community?

797
00:57:46.230 --> 00:57:47.034
<v WILLIAMS>Right, yes.

798
00:57:47.034 --> 00:57:50.253
That's been a great power point for Mossville.

799
00:57:50.253 --> 00:57:52.264
Whether it's good or bad.

800
00:57:52.264 --> 00:57:57.494
Well today they say block voting ain’t good but they still do it.

801
00:57:57.494 --> 00:57:58.299
It works.

802
00:57:58.299 --> 00:57:59.103
ARSENEAULT: Yes.

803
00:57:59.103 --> 00:57:59.506
Right.

804
00:57:59.506 --> 00:58:03.126
It's kind of how the world works, I guess.

805
00:58:03.126 --> 00:58:03.529
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah.

806
00:58:03.529 --> 00:58:05.138
So that's the school.

807
00:58:05.138 --> 00:58:07.552
And like I said, the churches.

808
00:58:07.552 --> 00:58:11.172
The recreation . . . That was the center points of Mossville.

809
00:58:11.172 --> 00:58:11.575
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees].

810
00:58:11.575 --> 00:58:18.414
<v WILLIAMS>This swimming pool you see right there, all of that came under my . . . When I was here.

811
00:58:18.414 --> 00:58:22.034
Like I say, thirty-something years they built that pool.

812
00:58:22.034 --> 00:58:25.655
I was one of the first to manage it.

813
00:58:25.655 --> 00:58:29.678
My wife came in the picture, and we worked together.

814
00:58:29.678 --> 00:58:32.494
But it was under water before then.

815
00:58:32.494 --> 00:58:42.149
See, they got the city running it now, it was under water before Sulphur [Louisiana] had it, all the recreation center and swimming pool.

816
00:58:42.149 --> 00:58:51.000
The kids were so poor, I talked the director of recreation water [?] to allow us to have a free swimming day.

817
00:58:51.000 --> 00:58:53.895
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, so you had to pay to get in?

818
00:58:53.895 --> 00:58:54.860
<v WILLIAMS>Everybody come free.

819
00:58:54.860 --> 00:58:57.112
<v ARSENEAULT>On one day out of the week?

820
00:58:57.112 --> 00:58:59.042
<v WILLIAMS>One day out of the week.

821
00:58:59.042 --> 00:59:01.615
I tell you what, they’d come from everywhere.

822
00:59:01.615 --> 00:59:02.259
<c.p>Ask Butch.

823
00:59:02.259 --> 00:59:02.580
Everywhere.

824
00:59:02.580 --> 00:59:05.476
That pool be . . . I had to have three lifeguards.

825
00:59:05.476 --> 00:59:08.049
Old and young swimming all day long, free.

826
00:59:08.049 --> 00:59:12.874
Some of them swim that morning, go home eat and come back that evening, see.

827
00:59:12.874 --> 00:59:16.091
<v ARSENEAULT>Get as much time as they can at the pool.

828
00:59:16.091 --> 00:59:16.413
<v WILLIAMS>Yeah.

829
00:59:16.413 --> 00:59:20.916
So eventually what they did, I think now they all swim free, I believe.

830
00:59:20.916 --> 00:59:28.958
But I started it here in Mossville, because I saw a lot of kids that a lot of families had six kids in one family.

831
00:59:28.958 --> 00:59:30.888
They couldn’t have that much money.

832
00:59:30.888 --> 00:59:33.783
They send some and some couldn't come or whatever.

833
00:59:33.783 --> 00:59:36.357
Well that was hurting to them, you see.

834
00:59:36.357 --> 00:59:37.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

835
00:59:37.000 --> 00:59:40.214
<v WILLIAMS>Here all these kids, they want to be together.

836
00:59:40.214 --> 00:59:40.571
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees].

837
00:59:40.571 --> 00:59:44.500
<v WILLIAMS>That was one of the great things that I helped do.

838
00:59:44.500 --> 00:59:45.214
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s wonderful.

839
00:59:45.214 --> 00:59:53.786
Thank you so much for sharing today, and on behalf of the center and the museum we appreciate you sharing all your beautiful stories.

840
00:59:53.786 --> 00:59:57.000
And we'll be in touch to follow up.

841
00:59:57.000 --> 00:59:57.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4431.

842
00:59:57.000 --> 00:59:57.200
End Session I.]
